
  <sn><md>### Why `rclone sync` Can Delete Files

The purpose of `rclone sync` is to make the **destination exactly match the source**.

Its basic rule is:

```text
SOURCE  ─────────────►  DESTINATION

Destination must become identical to the source.
```

**Example**

Before synchronization:

```text
SOURCE                       DESTINATION

📁 Documents                 📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt                 ├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt                 ├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt                 ├── 📄 C.txt
                             └── 📄 Old.txt ❌
```

Command:

```bash
rclone sync SOURCE DESTINATION
```

Rclone compares both locations.

```text
SOURCE                       DESTINATION

A.txt  ✔️ Exists              A.txt  ✔️ Keep
B.txt  ✔️ Exists              B.txt  ✔️ Keep
C.txt  ✔️ Exists              C.txt  ✔️ Keep
                              Old.txt ❌ Not in source
```

Since `Old.txt` does **not exist in the source**, rclone deletes it from the destination.

**After Synchronization**

```text
SOURCE                       DESTINATION

📁 Documents                 📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt                 ├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt                 ├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt                 └── 📄 C.txt
```

Now both locations are identical.
