<sn><md>### What Happens When You Delete a Source File?

Suppose initially:

```text
SOURCE                       DESTINATION

📄 A.txt                     📄 A.txt
📄 B.txt                     📄 B.txt
📄 C.txt                     📄 C.txt
```

You delete `B.txt` from the source:

```text
SOURCE                       DESTINATION

📄 A.txt                     📄 A.txt
❌ B.txt                     📄 B.txt
📄 C.txt                     📄 C.txt
```

Then you run:

```bash
rclone sync SOURCE DESTINATION
```

Rclone sees:

&gt; "`B.txt` exists in the destination but not in the source."

Therefore:

```text
SOURCE                       DESTINATION

📄 A.txt                     📄 A.txt
❌ B.txt                     ❌ B.txt
📄 C.txt                     📄 C.txt
```

</md></sn><sn><md>### The Simple Rule

```text
rclone sync SOURCE DESTINATION

Does it exist in SOURCE?
        │
        ├── ✔️ Yes → Keep or update it in DESTINATION
        │
        └── ❌ No → Delete it from DESTINATION
```

**Why Is This Useful?**

`sync` is useful when you want an exact mirror:

```text
💻 Local Folder  ═══════════► ☁️ Cloud Backup
```

For example, if you remove unnecessary files from your local folder, running `sync` ensures those files are also removed from the cloud mirror.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Important Warning

The command order matters:

```bash
rclone sync ~/Documents mega:Backup
```

This means:

```text
~/Documents ─────────────► mega:Backup

   SOURCE                   DESTINATION
                              ⚠️ Can be deleted from here
```

Before running an important sync, always check it safely:

```bash
rclone sync ~/Documents mega:Backup --dry-run
```

`--dry-run` shows which files would be copied, updated, or deleted without actually making any changes.
</md></sn>